[85.009238, "o", "142-the solder paste, and repeating it \r\n143-over and over. \r\n144-I WETTING ; ‘ TIME , \r\n145-30-60 .’ : SEC ~ \r\n146-TYPICAL PROFILE \r\n147:A typical profile consists of a preheat, dryout, and reflow \r\n148-section (zone) where different heating rates are used. In \r\n149:the preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT \r\n150-assembly to temperatures generally between 100°C to \r\n151:150°C. The most critical parameter in the preheat section \r\n152-is to minimize the rate of rise to less than 2”C/second. \r\n153-The main concern is minimizing thermal shock on the \r\n154-components of the assembly. Multilayer ceramic chip \r\n155-capacitors can be vulnerable to cracking if heated too \r\n156-fast. Solder paste can spatter also, if heated too fast. \r\n--\r\n206-veyors. If mesh conveyors are used, these zones are \r\n207-unnecessary unless the product width is quite wide. \r\n208-A second issue is the question of independent top and \r\n209-bottom heat control. A common conception in SMT \r\n210-200 c \r\n211:I PREHEAT1 I PREHEAT2 I REFLOW \r\n212-I \r\n213-I \r\n214-I \r\n215-I \r\n216-I \r\n--\r\n243-SOLDER JOINTS \r\n244-(TOO COLD) \r\n245-REFLOW SOLDERING DEFECTS \r\n246-If the product temperature profile is not maintained \r\n247-within the band, a defect will occur. Chip capacitors \r\n248:may crack if the rise rate in the preheat zone is exces- \r\n249-sive. Solder spatter and solder balls may occur if drying \r\n250-is excessive or insufficient. If drying is excessive, the \r\n251-solder paste may skin over and not allow any of the \r\n252-volatiles to escape. As the temperature of the solder joint \r\n253-continues to heat, the vapor pressure increases until the \r\n254-solder joint virtually explodes as the gases escape rap- \r\n255-idly. The result is solder spatter and solder balls. If the \r\n256:preheat is insufficient, solder spatter and balls may occur \r\n257-in the reflow zone when higher heating rates occur. It is \r\n258-critical that the paste is dried at low controlled heating \r\n259-rates. In the reflow zone, insufficient heat will result in \r\n260-non-reflowed or partially reflowed solder joints. The \r\n261-joints will have a dull, rough surface finish in this case. \r\n--\r\n922-the heater cavity flow diagram for the MICRI-FLO XG. \r\n923-The reflow and dryout zones each receive input gas \r\n924-(air or nitrogen) through a series of flow meters. This \r\n925-controlled flow enters a diffusion plenum behind the \r\n926-heating plenum to distribute the flow. The flow then \r\n927:enters a second plenum, where the gas is preheated by a \r\n928-series of heaters. The heaters consist of a wound nickel- \r\n929-chromium wire encased in a stainless steel jacket. The \r\n930-GAS FLOW INPUT FROM \r\n931-AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE \r\n932-II 4 Y \r\n--\r\n942-constant conditions. \r\n943-The flow is removed from the reflow area by two exhaust \r\n944-ducts located on each side of the reflow zone. This flow \r\n945-is pulled from the zones using a high temperature, low \r\n946-flow recirculating blower. The flow is pushed by the \r\n947:blower through the preheater plenum arrangement. The \r\n948-temperature control is accomplished in the same way as \r\n949-the dryout and reflow zone. \r\n950-The flow that heats the product (in all zones) is forced \r\n951-through the walls in an array of holes to break up the \r\n952-boundary layers on the product. The perpendicular flow \r\n--\r\n989-where the product temperature is greater than 150°C, \r\n990-pure nitrogen is delivered to the reflow and dryout \r\n991-zone. This is a key point, as this ensures that the product \r\n992-experiences a nearly pure environment at its most criti- \r\n993-cal stages (dryout and reflow). The recirculated gas \r\n994:from these zones is reheated and used in the preheat zone. \r\n995-Here, purity is not as critical, so the use of recirculated \r\n996-gas is not detrimental to the process. \r\n997-Purity levels in the dryout and reflow zone can be \r\n998-maintained at 35 PPM over incoming purity levels at \r\n999-flow rates of 1800 cubic feet per hour (5 1 cubic meters \r\n--\r\n1089-decreases of under 40°C. Even in the worst case of going \r\n1090-from a reflow profile to an adhesive cure profile, the \r\n1091-changeover time is under 15 minutes. \r\n1092"]
[85.009275, "o", "-Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology 17 \r\n1093-IR/CONVECTION OVEN WITH NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE \r\n1094:PREHEAT TOP (IR1) \r\n1095-Infrared ovens do not require high quantities of flow to \r\n1096-accomplish reflow. The predominant heat transfer oc- \r\n1097-curs with IR, which does not require any flow to transfer \r\n1098-the heat. The flow in the oven is used to provide some of \r\n1099-the benefits of convection, which includes improving \r\n--\r\n1116-sections are present to provide a nitrogen curtain at the \r\n1117-chamber entrance and exit, which also assists in keeping \r\n1118-oxygen out of the chamber. Flow meter controlled \r\n1119-venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow in the \r\n"]
