[109.380948, "o", "162-plished in the reflow zone, where the solder paste is \r\n163-elevated to a temperature greater than the melting point \r\n164-of the solder. For 63/37 Eutectic solder, the melting \r\n165-temperature is 183°C. This temperature must be ex- \r\n166-ceeded by approximately 20°C to ensure quality reflow. \r\n167:The amount of time the solder joint is above the melting \r\n168-point is referred to as the “wetting time”. The wetting \r\n169-time is normally 30 to 60 seconds for most pastes. If the \r\n170-wetting time is excessive, intermetallics may occur in \r\n171-the joint, which result in brittle solder joints. In sum- \r\n172-mary, define the profile, and hit it. \r\n"]
[109.381155, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@e00a671c414b:/app# "]
[114.158455, "i", "grep -n -i -A 5 -B 5 'conveyor\\|dwell' /tmp/handbook.txt | head -150\n"]
[114.165004, "o", "grep -n -i -A 5 -B 5 'conveyor\\|dwell' /tmp/handbook.txt | head -150\r\n"]
[114.165224, "o", "\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[114.167306, "o", "22-Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere ............................................................................................... 16 \r\n23-IR/Convection Oven Closed Loop Control ................................................................................................... 17 \r\n24-References ..................................................................................................................................................... 19 \r\n25-Product Matrix .................................................................................................................. ; ............................ 20 \r\n26-IR/Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere .......................................................................................... 18 \r\n27:Edge Conveyor .............................................................................................................................................. 19 \r\n28-. \r\n29-. \r\n30-. \r\n31-. \r\n32-Printed in the U.S.A. 0 Research. Inc., 1992 1 \r\n--\r\n198-Product warpage may also be minimized through proper \r\n199-control. The bottom line is that most machines have this \r\n200-feature, but most often the top and bottom heaters will be \r\n201-set at the same value. \r\n202-Edge heating zones become significant when edge con- \r\n203:veyors are used. Edge conveyors act as heat sinks on the \r\n204-edge of the product. Two zones of edge heat (one front, \r\n205-one back) will assist in compensating for edge con- \r\n206:veyors. If mesh conveyors are used, these zones are \r\n207-unnecessary unless the product width is quite wide. \r\n208-A second issue is the question of independent top and \r\n209-bottom heat control. A common conception in SMT \r\n210-200 c \r\n211-I PREHEAT1 I PREHEAT2 I REFLOW \r\n--\r\n298-WHAT ARE THE CAUSES OF NON-UNIFORM HEATING? \r\n299-In order to fully comprehend the capability to heat within \r\n300-the defined process band, one needs to better understand \r\n301-the causes of heating non-uniformities. The three main \r\n302-causes of non-uniform heating in the SMT reflow pro- \r\n303:cess include mass differential on the product, conveyor \r\n304-and heater edge effects, and product loading. \r\n305-MASS DIFFERENTIAL \r\n306-The amount of temperature rise of a product as it travels \r\n307-through a heating zone depends upon a number of \r\n308-variables. The temperature rise of an object subjected to \r\n--\r\n310-AT = Q x AX t / (M x Cp) \r\n311-WHERE: \r\n312-AT = Product Temperature Rise (“C) \r\n313-Q = Heat Absorbed (w/cmz) \r\n314-A = Exposed Area (cm2) \r\n315:t = Heating Dwell Time (seconds) \r\n316-M = Mass of Object (kg) \r\n317-Cp = Specific Heat (w-sec/kg-”C) \r\n318-The mass (M) times the specific heat (Cp) is often referred \r\n319-to as thermal mass. Greater thermal mass requires more \r\n320-heat to achieve a given temperature rise. In the figure \r\n--\r\n335-mmmm \r\n336-OVEN UNIFORMITY \r\n337-A second main cause of non-uniform product heating is \r\n338-the oven heating pattern. This can be caused by edge \r\n339-effects on the heaters (less heat at the end) or by heat \r\n340:sinking of the conveyor system. The edge effect may be \r\n341-caused by insufficient airflow around the edges of the \r\n342-machine, or by non-uniformities that are inherent in the \r\n343:heaters. The heat sinking of the conveyor is really amass \r\n344-effect, similar to the one described above. The oven \r\n345-uniformity may be measured by thermocoupling a bare \r\n346-board over a matrix of points, and running a profile. The \r\n347-matrix should include points on the board edges (front, \r\n348-back, left, and right), and the center of the board. \r\n--\r\n365-running a profile on a test board, then load the oven \r\n366-down. Periodically, run the test board within the oven \r\n367-load and compare with the unloaded profile. \r\n368-The three main causes of non-uniform heating in \r\n369-the SMT reflow process include mass differential \r\n370:on the product, conveyor and heater edge effects, \r\n371-and product loading. \r\n372-6 Section I - Technology \r\n373-WHAT ARE THE MODES OF HEATING? \r\n374-There are three different heating modes involved wit \r"]
[114.167613, "o", "\n375-most SMT reflow processes; convection, conductior \r\n--\r\n749-The oven must be sized to handle the production rate of \r\n750-the pick-and-place equipment. The calculation is shown \r\n751-below: \r\n752-LINE SPEED - BOARDS PER MINUTE X LENGTH PER BOARD \r\n753-(MINIMUM) - LOAD FACTOR \r\n754:Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated \r\n755-speed ensures that no product jams will occur on the \r\n756-onload area of the reflow oven. \r\n757-Try to avoid becoming too focussed on one \r\n758-issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may \r\n759-not lead you to the optimum decision. \r\n--\r\n762-calculated line speed. This will ensure that bottlenecks \r\n763-will not occur at the reflow oven. The process speed \r\n764-should be determined through testing or by the following \r\n765-equation: \r\n766-OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH PROCESS - \r\n767:SPEED - PROCESS DWELL TIME \r\n768-DEFINE PROCESS GAS \r\n769-If a nitrogen atmosphere is required for solderability or \r\n770-no-clean processing (see page lo), one needs to consider \r\n771-the purity required and the gas consumption. Be careful \r\n772-how the purity is measured, such that it is not measured \r\n773-at the gas inlet. \r\n774:REVIEW CONVEYOR DESIGN \r\n775-The conveying mechanism is perhaps the most over- \r\n776-looked aspect of equipment selection. One needs to \r\n777:define whether a mesh conveyor or an edge type is \r\n778:required. Typically edge conveyors are used for in-line \r\n779-processing or if double-sided assembly is required. The \r\n780:most important issue with edge conveyors is the ability \r\n781:for the conveyor to remain straight to avoid dropping \r\n782-product. \r\n783-RELIABILITY/SERVICEABILITY \r\n784-Once a machine is placed into service, it is critical that it \r\n785-remains operational. Evaluate the product design from a \r\n786-structural as well as a functional standpoint. Is the oven \r\n--\r\n788-the product manuals, drawings, and spare parts lists. \r\n789-Find out where the nearest servicing center is located. Is \r\n790-training available? These are the questions that must be \r\n791-asked during the selection process. \r\n792-INTERFACING REQUIREMENTS \r\n793:Conveyor and control interfacing requirements may also \r\n794-be an important selection issue. Make sure that the edge \r\n795:conveyor fixed rail justification is the same as the pick- \r\n796-and-place equipment. Ask questions about networking \r\n797-machines to a host and product flow control schemes if \r\n798-needed. \r\n799-12 Section I1 - Equipment Selection \r\n800-HOW CAN YOU EVALUATE COSTS? \r\n--\r\n889-PROCESS GAS TYPE \r\n890-DRYOUT ZONE PURITY (PPM) \r\n891-I PROCESS GAS FLOW \r\n892-POWER REQUIRED \r\n893-UNDER PRODUCTION LOAD (KW) \r\n894:CONVEYOR TYPE \r\n895:CONVEYOR STRAIGHTNESS \r\n896-(IF EDGE) \r\n897:CONVEYOR DIRECTION/ \r\n898-RAIL JUSTIFICATION \r\n899-GENERAL MACHINE QUALITY \r\n900-RATING (1-10) \r\n901-MANUALS AND DOCUMENTATION \r\n902-RATING (1-10) \r\n--\r\n1142-which is considerably less than in the case \r\n1143-of a convection oven. \r\n1144-NOTE: VENTURI EXHAUSTS ARE ACTIVATED BY COMPRESSED AIR, \r\n1145-ALL OTHERS MAY BE NITROGEN OR COMPRESSED AIR. \r\n1146-18 Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \r\n"]
