[122.036959, "o", "314-A = Exposed Area (cm2) \r\n315-t = Heating Dwell Time (seconds) \r\n316-M = Mass of Object (kg) \r\n317-Cp = Specific Heat (w-sec/kg-”C) \r\n318-The mass (M) times the specific heat (Cp) is often referred \r\n319:to as thermal mass. Greater thermal mass requires more \r\n320-heat to achieve a given temperature rise. In the figure \r\n321-shown below, the area populated with the PLCC’s has \r\n322:considerably more thermal mass per unit area than the \r\n323-area with the discrete components. This area will be more \r\n324-difficult to heat. The non-uniformity caused by thermal \r\n325-mass differential may be measured by thermocoupling a \r\n326-lead on the largest and smallest device, and running a \r\n327-profile. \r\n--\r\n596-efficiency and reduce the amount of process gas required \r\n597-CONVECTION \r\n598-Less sensitive to shading \r\n599-Heats more uniformly \r\n600-AT (Source-target) is small \r\n601:Low thermal mass of air requires \r\n602-high flow rates to transfer heat \r\n603-Inefficient (20 to 30%) \r\n604-Will heat leads first, increasing \r\n605-chance of solder wicking. \r\n606-is through the use of recirculation. In a recirculation \r\n--\r\n1046-equilibrium. This rapid response is ideal for \r\n1047-closed loop control, as energy can be replaced \r\n1048-in the heating chamber instantly after its \r\n1049-removal. \r\n1050-RAPID-RESPONSE (EXPOSED-TIP) \r\n1051:THERMOCOUPLE \r\n1052-PRODUCT \r\n1053-”‘7 \\ REFLF JON. $ RE-EMITTED IR \r\n1054-VECTION 1 FLOW \r\n1055--- \\P \r\n1056-PRODUCT \r\n"]
